refer superlunary.Rig

entry .Bench   a benchmark harness: times a quoted operation in its own JVM, singly or across one or two axes
  detail
      A `Bench` is a superlunary `Rig` which stages quoted code into a JAR, deploys it to the
      `BenchmarkDevice` in scope, and times it there. It is constructed once, usually at the
      top of a suite's `run()`, and applied to a name and settings to give a `Bench.Plan`, which
      is applied in turn to a quoted body, or spread `over` one or two axes:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import superlunary.embeddings.automaticEmbedding
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      given BenchmarkDevice = LocalhostDevice

      enum Summation:
        case Loop, Formula

      object Benchmarks extends Suite(m"Summation benchmarks"):
        def run(): Unit =
          val bench = Bench()

          bench(m"sum of the first thousand integers")
            ( target = 50*Milli(Second), baseline = Summation.Formula )
          . over(Summation):
              case Summation.Loop =>
                ' {
                    var i = 1L
                    var sum = 0L
                    while i <= 1000L do
                      sum += i
                      i += 1L
                    sum
                  }

              case Summation.Formula =>
                '{1000L*1001L/2L}
      ```

      Each cell is measured in the same way. The body is bound once as a method of its own,
      run ten times untimed, and then run in batches of doubling size until one batch takes at
      least `target/iterations`; `warmups` full batches follow, adjusting the batch size towards
      that duration, and the median of their rates fixes it. Then `iterations` timed batches
      run, with a garbage collection requested between each, and the allocation over them is
      read from the JVM's thread-allocation counter. Each operation's result is written to a
      sink the JIT cannot see through, so a body whose value is unused is not eliminated; a body
      which still measures nothing after 2³⁴ repetitions fails, as suspected dead code. The
      result of each cell is a probably `Benchmark`: total and mean time, minimum, maximum,
      standard deviation, the confidence interval, bytes allocated per operation and, given an
      `OperationSize`, the size and rate of an operation.

      The constructor's parameters pin the measurement JVM. `heap` sets both `-Xms` and `-Xmx`
      (in `-Xmx` syntax, `t"256m"`; the default is a fixed `1g`); `cpus` limits it to that many
      processors (see `BenchmarkDevice#invoke` for what that does on each platform); and `gc`
      names the collector in `-XX:+Use<gc>GC` syntax (`t"G1"`, `t"Z"`; the default is
      `Serial`, whose single-threaded, deterministic pauses suit microbenchmarks). A body which
      runs a fiber runtime or many virtual threads should ask for `gc = t"G1"` and a heap sized
      to what a rival's own harness used.

      A `Bench` needs a `Classloader`, an `Environment` and a `BenchmarkDevice` where it is
      constructed. Benchmark rows are the ones fume's `--bench` switch selects.
  refer sedentary.Bench.Plan
  refer sedentary.BenchmarkDevice
  refer sedentary.OperationSize
  refer probably.Benchmark
  entry .Plan   a named, configured benchmark, awaiting its quoted body or the axes to spread it over
    detail
        A `Plan` is what `Bench#apply` returns: the benchmark's name, tags and settings, with
        the `Bench` which will run it. It is used in one of three ways:

        - applied directly to a quoted body, `plan('{ … })`, for a single measurement;
        - spread `over` one or two axes, with a body which maps each axis value (or pair of
          values) to a quoted body, for one measurement per value — rendered as a row of
          cells, or a crosstab for two axes;
        - given a per-cell `OperationSize` with `sized` first, and then spread `over`.

        Watched as a plan grows: a single measurement, then one per implementation, then one
        per implementation and input, each cell sized by its input.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        frame
          label A single measurement
          code
              bench(m"parse a document")(target = 1*Second):
                '{ Parser.parse(Corpus.small) }
        frame
          label One measurement per implementation, against a baseline
          code
              bench(m"parse a document")(target = 1*Second, baseline = Implementation.Ours)
              . over(Implementation):
                  case Implementation.Ours  => '{ Parser.parse(Corpus.small) }
                  case Implementation.Rival => '{ RivalParser.parse(Corpus.small) }
        frame
          label One per implementation and document, each sized by its document
          code
              bench(m"parse a document")(target = 1*Second, baseline = Implementation.Ours)
              . sized: (_: Implementation, document: Document) =>
                  val size: OperationSize = Corpus.text(document).length*Byte
                  size
              . over(Axis(Implementation), Axis(Document)):
                  case (Implementation.Ours, document) =>
                    val text: Text = Corpus.text(document)
                    '{ Parser.parse($text) }
                  case (Implementation.Rival, document) =>
                    val text: Text = Corpus.text(document)
                    '{ RivalParser.parse($text) }
        ##
        ```

        Every cell of a plan is staged and dispatched on its own, but cells whose staged trees
        coincide — those differing only in a value spliced in with `$` — share one
        compilation, and each distinct implementation compiles once. A value spliced into the
        body travels through superlunary's `References`, and is extracted once per run.
    entry .Uniaxial   a plan whose cells over one axis are each given their own `OperationSize`
      detail
          What `Plan#sized` returns for a function of one value. Its `#over` measures every
          defined value of an axis, sizing each cell by `#size`; a cell for which the function
          answers `Unset` falls back to the plan's own `operationSize`.
      entry #plan   the plan whose settings each cell takes
      entry #size   the size of the cell for an axis value, or `Unset` for the plan's own
      entry #over   one measurement per defined value of an axis, each sized by `#size`
          As `Bench.Plan#over` for one axis. The body is a partial function from the axis value
          to a quoted body: a value for which it is not defined is a gap in the row, probed
          without staging anything. If the plan has a `baseline` among the axis's values, that
          cell is reported as the anchor the others are compared against.
    entry .Biaxial   a plan whose crosstab cells are each given their own `OperationSize`
      detail
          What `Plan#sized` returns for a function of two values. Its `#over` measures every
          defined combination of two axes, sizing each cell by `#size`, with the plan's own
          `operationSize` wherever the function answers `Unset`.
      entry #plan   the plan whose settings each cell takes
      entry #size   the size of the cell for a pair of axis values, or `Unset` for the plan's own
      entry #over   one measurement per defined combination of two axes, each sized by `#size`
          As `Bench.Plan#over` for two axes. The body is a partial function from a pair of axis
          values to a quoted body, so a combination which makes no sense is left as a gap. A
          `baseline` is looked for among the first axis's values, and then the second's.
    entry #bench   the `Bench` which stages and runs the plan's measurements
    entry #name   the benchmark's name, as a `Message`
    entry #tags   the tags which label the benchmark for selection and exclusion, such as `slow`
    entry #target   the target measuring time, as a `Long` count of nanoseconds
        The `target` given to `Bench#apply`, as the generic duration. It is divided among the
        timed batches, and scaled by the runner's duration multiplier before it is used.
    entry #operationSize   the size of one operation, for a throughput rate, or `Unset`
    entry #iterations   how many timed batches each cell runs: five unless set
    entry #warmups   how many full calibration batches precede the timed ones: as many as `#iterations` unless set
    entry #confidence   the percentile of the reported confidence interval: 95 unless set
    entry #anchor   the axis value whose cell the others are compared against, or `Unset`
    entry #comparison   how the other cells are compared with the anchor: a probably `Baseline`
        Which statistic is compared (`Min`, `Mean` or `Max`), as a rate or a time, and whether
        the comparison is geometric or arithmetic; `Baseline()` compares geometric mean rates.
    entry #apply   a single measurement of a quoted body
        `plan('{ … })` stages the body, runs it on the plan's device and includes one
        `Benchmark` in the report, unless the runner has not selected it, in which case
        nothing is staged. The body is a quoted expression of any type, built with superlunary's
        `References` and the staging `Quotes` in context.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        bench(m"decode directly")(target = 1*Second, operationSize = 1000*Byte):
          '{ Benchmarks.decodeUsersDirect() }
        ```
    entry #sized   gives each cell its own `OperationSize`, by its axis value or values
        `plan.sized((value: A) => …)` returns a `Bench.Plan.Uniaxial`, and `plan.sized((left:
        A, right: B) => …)` a `Bench.Plan.Biaxial`, whose `over` then measures the axes. A
        parser measured over documents of different lengths reports each document's size, so
        that the cells' rates are comparable where their times are not. A cell for which the
        function answers `Unset` uses the plan's `operationSize`. The lambda's parameter types
        must be written, so that the overload is chosen.
    entry #over   one measurement per defined value of an axis, or per combination of two
        `plan.over(axis)(body)` takes an `Axis` (`Axis(t"limit")(1000, 4000)`) and a partial
        function from each of its values to a quoted body; `plan.over(first, second)(body)`
        takes two axes and a partial function of pairs, and renders a crosstab. An enumeration's
        companion may stand for an axis of all its cases — `plan.over(Summation)` — in either
        position. A value or pair for which the body is not defined leaves a gap, found without
        staging anything, and a cell the runner has not selected is skipped before it is staged.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        bench(m"count up to a limit")(target = 50*Milli(Second))
        . over(Axis(t"limit")(1000, 4000)): limit =>
            ' {
                var i = 0
                while i < $limit do i += 1
                i
              }
        ```

        Here both cells share one compilation, since they differ only in the spliced `limit`.
        Every cell shares the plan's `operationSize`; `#sized` gives each its own.
  entry .Error   raised when a benchmark device cannot deploy, run or remove a measurement
    detail
        Error 794: _unable to run benchmarks_. Raised by a `BenchmarkDevice` when copying the
        staged JAR to the device, launching the measurement JVM, or removing the JAR fails, or
        when the measurement JVM exits with a nonzero status. `Bench`, `Stress` and `Profile`
        convert it to superlunary's `Rig.Error`, with the reason `Staging` or `Invocation`, so a
        suite sees a `Rig.Error`.

        `LocalhostDevice` passes the staged input to the measurement JVM as a command-line
        argument, so a body which splices in a very large value (a corpus of a megabyte or
        more) can exceed the operating system's argument-length limit and fail with this error.
  entry #heap   the measurement JVM's fixed heap size, as `-Xmx` text, or `Unset` for `1g`
  entry #cpus   how many processors the measurement JVM may use, or `Unset` for all of them
  entry #gc   the measurement JVM's collector, as in `-XX:+Use<gc>GC`, or `Unset` for `Serial`
  entry #Result   the result type of a staged invocation: the output itself
  entry #Form   the form staged inputs and outputs travel in: `Text`
  entry #Target   what staging produces: the `Path on Linux` of the deployed JAR
  entry #Transport   how spliced values are encoded for the measurement JVM: `Json`
  entry #apply   names and configures a benchmark, returning a `Bench.Plan` to apply to a body
      `bench(name, tags*)(target, operationSize, iterations, warmups, confidence, baseline,
      comparison)`:

      - `name` is a `Message`, `m"…"`, and `tags` label the benchmark for selection
        (`n"slow"`, which needs the tag plane's `Nominative` imported by name:
        `import soundness.{nominative, taggingNominative}`);
      - `target` is the measuring time, any duration (`1*Second`, `50*Milli(Second)`);
      - `operationSize` the size of one operation, usually a quantity such as `4096*Byte`,
        converted by `OperationSize.conversion`, for a throughput figure;
      - `iterations` the number of timed batches (five by default), and `warmups` the number
        of calibration batches (as many as `iterations` by default);
      - `confidence` the percentile of the confidence interval: 80, 85, 90, 95 (the default),
        96, 97, 98 or 99;
      - `baseline` an axis value whose cell anchors a comparison, for a plan spread `over` an
        axis, and `comparison` how the comparison is made, a probably `Baseline`.
  entry #stage   builds the code under measurement into a JAR and deploys it to the device
      Bundles the staged code with superlunary's executor as its entry point, names the JAR by
      a fresh UUID, and calls the device's `deploy`. A failure to build or deploy is raised as
      a `Rig.Error` with the reason `Staging`. `Bench`, `Stress` and `Profile` all stage the same
      way; a suite never calls this itself.

entry .Stress   a stress test: a quoted operation run concurrently for a window, reporting memory, GC and latency
  detail
      Where a `Bench` times one operation repeated on a single thread, a `Stress` runs the
      operation on `concurrency` workers at once, each repeating it until a wall-clock window
      closes, and reports what a throughput figure hides, as a probably `Strain`: the worker
      count, the operations completed and the time taken, total allocation (and so allocation
      per operation), peak heap, the live set retained after a garbage collection, the number
      and duration of collections, and the 50th, 90th, 99th and 99.9th percentile latencies
      of an operation, from a log-linear histogram accurate to about 3%.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import superlunary.embeddings.automaticEmbedding
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory
      import threads.virtualThreads

      given BenchmarkDevice = LocalhostDevice

      object Benchmarks extends Suite(m"Hand-off benchmarks"):
        def run(): Unit =
          val stress = Stress()
          val constrained = Stress(heap = t"128m")

          stress(m"cross-thread hand-off")(target = 2*Second, concurrency = 16):
            '{ Pipeline.handOff() }

          constrained(m"hand-off in a small heap")(target = 1*Second, sweep = 1024):
            '{ Pipeline.handOff() }
      ```

      A single window is one measurement. Three settings of `#apply` turn it into a search
      over the worker count, each window's count chosen by a `StressSearch` from the outcome of
      the windows before it, and every window reported as a cell on the test's `N` axis, so a
      search reads as a curve against `N` in one test rather than as many tests:

      - `sweep` doubles the worker count from `concurrency` (one, by default) up to `sweep`,
        stopping early at a window which ran out of memory or spent more than half its time
        collecting garbage — so in a constrained heap the last row is the most concurrency
        that heap sustains;
      - `threshold` and `compliance`, given together, search for **capacity**: the largest
        worker count at which at least `compliance` percent of operations complete within
        `threshold`, binary-searched to about 12% and then confirmed over a window three times
        longer, the confirmed row flagged `sustained`;
      - `refine` looks for the **optimum** count rather than a power of two or the largest
        compliant count: the smallest whose throughput is within 5% of the best — the peak of
        a peaked curve, or the knee of one which flattens out — re-measured over extended
        windows and flagged `sustained`.

      The workers' threads are the kind the `Threading` in scope forks: under
      `threads.virtualThreads`, a high-concurrency measurement multiplexes its workers over the
      carrier pool, as a massively concurrent application would. Allocation is still fully
      accounted either way. A worker whose operation throws fails its window, reported once
      on the standard error, rather than printing a trace per worker.

      The constructor's `heap`, `cpus` and `gc` pin the measurement JVM as for `Bench`; a
      saturated multi-threaded workload measuring tail latency should select `gc = t"G1"`, so
      that the Serial collector's stop-the-world pauses do not dominate the 99th percentile.
      Stress rows are selected with fume's `--stress` switch, not `--bench`.
  refer sedentary.StressSearch
  refer probably.Strain
  refer sedentary.Bench
  entry #heap   the measurement JVM's fixed heap size, as `-Xmx` text, or `Unset` for `1g`
  entry #cpus   how many processors the measurement JVM may use, or `Unset` for all of them
  entry #gc   the measurement JVM's collector, as in `-XX:+Use<gc>GC`, or `Unset` for `Serial`
  entry #Result   the result type of a staged invocation: the output itself
  entry #Form   the form staged inputs and outputs travel in: `Text`
  entry #Target   what staging produces: the `Path on Linux` of the deployed JAR
  entry #Transport   how spliced values are encoded for the measurement JVM: `Json`
  entry #apply   runs a stress test of a quoted body: one window, a sweep, a capacity search or a refinement
      `stress(name, tags*)(target, concurrency, sweep, threshold, compliance, refine)(body)`:

      - `target` is the length of each window, any duration, scaled by the runner's duration
        multiplier; `threshold`, a latency criterion rather than a length of time, is not;
      - `concurrency` is the worker count, or the first count of a search (one by default);
      - `sweep` is the largest worker count a search may reach (1024 for a capacity search
        without one);
      - `threshold` and `compliance` (a percentage) together set a latency objective; giving
        only one of them is a programming error, and panics;
      - `refine` searches for the optimum worker count.

      The body is a quoted expression, as for `Bench.Plan#apply`. The test declares its `N`
      axis, bounded by `concurrency` and `sweep`, before anything runs, so a host can offer
      `N` constraints in a listing, and its expected duration is one window for a single
      measurement and roughly logarithmic in `sweep` for a search. A window which ran out of
      memory is not reported; every other window is, including a failed confirmation.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      stress(m"requests within 5 ms")
        ( target = 1*Second, threshold = 5*Milli(Second), compliance = 99, refine = true ):
        '{ Server.roundtrip() }
      ```
  entry #stage   builds the code under measurement into a JAR and deploys it to the device
      As `Bench#stage`: a failure is a `Rig.Error` with the reason `Staging`.

entry .StressSearch   chooses the worker count of each window of a `Stress` search, from the windows before it
  detail
      The search a `Stress` with `sweep`, `threshold` and `compliance`, or `refine` performs. It
      runs inside the measurement JVM, called from the staged loop, but is an ordinary class,
      so a search can be tested against a simulated workload without staging anything:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val search = StressSearch(1, 64, false, false)
      var counts: List[Int] = Nil

      while !search.done do
        counts = counts :+ search.current
        search.record(search.current.toDouble, true, false)

      counts   // List(1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64)
      ```

      The constructor takes the first worker count, the largest, whether there is a latency
      objective (an SLO), and whether to refine. Every search begins with an **ascent**,
      doubling the count, landing exactly on the cap even when it is not a power of two:

      - Without an SLO, the ascent is the whole of a plain sweep, stopping at the cap or at a
        window which exhausted memory or thrashed. A plain sweep has no winner.
      - With an SLO, the ascent stops at the first window which misses the objective, and the
        boundary between the largest compliant and the smallest non-compliant count is
        binary-searched to about 12%. The largest compliant count is confirmed over an extended
        window, stepping down about 12% at a time, up to three times, if the confirmation fails.
        If even the first window misses, the search stops there, with no winner.
      - With `refine`, after the ascent (and any boundary search) it probes halfway across
        the gaps around the fastest feasible count until they are within about 6% — first
        the **peak**, then the **knee**, the smallest count within `.Plateau` of the fastest —
        and re-measures the knee, the count below it and the peak over extended windows. The
        smallest of them within 5% of the fastest re-measurement wins. If the peak's
        re-measurement falls more than 5% short, the peak was a lucky window: the
        re-measurements replace the first ones and the search starts again, up to `.Restarts`
        times. A window which failed or missed the SLO bounds the probing but is never a
        candidate.
  refer sedentary.Stress
  entry .MaxProbes   the most windows spent probing around the peak, and again around the knee: 12
  entry .Plateau   the fraction of the best throughput within which a count is as good as the best: 0.05
  entry .Restarts   how many times a refined search starts again after finding a lucky peak: 2
  entry .Phase   the stage a search is in, from its ascent to done
      `Ascent` doubles the count; `Boundary` binary-searches the SLO boundary; `Peak` and
      `Knee` probe for the optimum in a refined search; `Confirmation` re-measures candidates
      over extended windows; `StepDown` retreats about 12% at a time from a failed
      confirmation; and `Done` ends the search.
  entry #done   whether the search has finished, so no further window should run
  entry #current   the worker count the next window should run at
  entry #extended   whether the next window is a confirmation, run three times longer than the target
  entry #winner   the ordinal of the winning window, counting every recorded window from zero, or -1
      A plain sweep, and a search in which no confirmation held, have no winner. A `Stress`
      flags the winning window's row `sustained`.
  entry #record   records the outcome of the window just run at `#current` workers
      `record(throughput, ok, exhausted)`: the window's throughput in operations per
      nanosecond; whether it was feasible — no failure, no memory exhaustion, no thrashing,
      and within the SLO if there is one; and whether it ran out of memory or thrashed, which
      ends a plain sweep's ascent. The search then chooses the next `#current`, or finishes.

entry .Profile   a profile: a quoted operation run under JFR, reporting its hottest methods as a histogram
  detail
      Where a `Bench` measures how fast an operation runs and a `Stress` how it scales, a
      `Profile` measures where its time goes. The body runs repeatedly on one thread for the
      target duration while an in-process JFR recording samples execution every millisecond,
      recording native-method samples alongside, so a pipeline bound by native code (zlib, say)
      is not invisible. Each sample's top frame — the method actually executing, its **self
      time** — is counted, and the hottest methods are reported as a probably `Hotspots`,
      rendered as a histogram with methods coloured by package.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import superlunary.embeddings.automaticEmbedding
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      given BenchmarkDevice = LocalhostDevice

      object Benchmarks extends Suite(m"Pipeline profiles"):
        def run(): Unit =
          val profile = Profile()

          profile(m"pipeline hotspots")(target = 5*Second):
            '{ Pipeline.run() }

          profile(m"pipeline costs, inclusive")(target = 5*Second, frames = 60, inclusive = true):
            '{ Pipeline.run() }
      ```

      JFR's execution sampler observes only runnable threads, so this is a CPU profile: time
      spent parked or waiting does not appear. It samples every thread, though, so the threads
      a body spawns are profiled with it. The constructor's `heap` and `cpus` pin the
      measurement JVM as for `Bench`; a profile always uses the device's default collector.
      Profile rows are selected with fume's `--profile` switch: `--bench` alone does not run
      them.
  refer probably.Hotspots
  refer sedentary.Bench
  entry #heap   the measurement JVM's fixed heap size, as `-Xmx` text, or `Unset` for `1g`
  entry #cpus   how many processors the measurement JVM may use, or `Unset` for all of them
  entry #Result   the result type of a staged invocation: the output itself
  entry #Form   the form staged inputs and outputs travel in: `Text`
  entry #Target   what staging produces: the `Path on Linux` of the deployed JAR
  entry #Transport   how spliced values are encoded for the measurement JVM: `Json`
  entry #apply   profiles a quoted body for a target duration, reporting its hottest methods
      `profile(name, tags*)(target, frames, inclusive)(body)`: `target` is how long to record
      for, any duration, scaled by the runner's duration multiplier; `frames` caps how many
      methods are reported (25 by default); and `inclusive` counts a sample for every distinct
      method on its stack rather than its top frame alone.

      An inclusive profile gives each method's share of the time spent in it **or anything it
      called**, which finds a cost hidden inside a callee or spread across several — a string
      constructor inlined into a generated loop, say — that the self-time histogram attributes
      elsewhere. The shares then sum to more than the whole, and the outermost frames show
      near 100%. Method and class names are reported in their source form, with compiler
      mangling undone.
  entry #stage   builds the code under measurement into a JAR and deploys it to the device
      As `Bench#stage`: a failure is a `Rig.Error` with the reason `Staging`.

entry .BenchmarkDevice   where a measurement JVM runs: the local machine, or a host reached over SSH
  detail
      A `BenchmarkDevice` deploys a staged JAR, launches a JVM to run it with a given input,
      returns its output, and removes the JAR. `Bench`, `Stress` and `Profile` each take one as
      a contextual value when they are constructed, so a suite chooses its device once, as a
      given:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given BenchmarkDevice = LocalhostDevice
      ```

      `LocalhostDevice` runs the JVM on the local machine; a `NetworkDevice` copies the JAR to
      a remote host with `scp` and runs it with `ssh`, and its `session` multiplexes every
      operation over one SSH connection. A device on a quiet, dedicated Linux machine gives
      the steadiest figures, and is the only kind on which `cpus` pins the process to cores.
      Every operation raises `Bench.Error` when it fails.

      The measurement JVM is launched with a fixed heap (`-Xms` and `-Xmx` both `1g` unless a
      `heap` is given), its heap pre-touched, two JIT compiler threads, and the Serial
      collector unless a `gc` is given.
  refer sedentary.LocalhostDevice
  refer sedentary.NetworkDevice
  refer sedentary.Bench.Error
  entry #deploy   makes a staged JAR available on the device, under a name from its UUID
      Raises `Bench.Error` if the JAR cannot be copied there.
  entry #invoke   runs a staged JAR in a fresh JVM on the device with one input, and returns its output
      `invoke(path, input, heap, cpus, gc)` raises `Bench.Error` if the JVM cannot be launched
      or exits with a nonzero status.

      - `heap` overrides the fixed heap size, in `-Xmx` syntax (`t"256m"`), for both `-Xms`
        and `-Xmx`; unset, it is `1g`. A constrained heap shows what a workload sustains in a
        small, pinned amount of memory.
      - `cpus` limits the JVM to that many processors with `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount`, which
        sizes the GC and JIT threads and every pool derived from the available-processor
        count. On a Linux `NetworkDevice` the process is additionally pinned to that many
        cores with `taskset`; elsewhere, including on a macOS `LocalhostDevice`, platform
        threads still schedule across every core, so the limit is advisory.
      - `gc` names the collector in `-XX:+Use<gc>GC` syntax (`t"G1"`, `t"Parallel"`, `t"Z"`);
        unset, it is `Serial`.
  entry #undeploy   removes a staged JAR from the device
      Raises `Bench.Error` if the JAR cannot be removed.

entry .LocalhostDevice   the benchmark device which runs each measurement JVM on the local machine
  detail
      Deploying and removing a JAR are no-ops, since the staged JAR is already on the local
      filesystem, and `invoke` launches the `java` on the path directly. The measurement JVM's
      standard error is inherited, so its diagnostics — a failed worker's stack trace — appear
      as they happen, and cannot fill an undrained pipe and stall the harness.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given BenchmarkDevice = LocalhostDevice
      ```

      The staged input reaches the JVM as a command-line argument, so a body which splices in
      a value of a megabyte or more may exceed the operating system's argument-length limit,
      and fail with `Bench.Error`. A `cpus` limit is advisory on macOS, which has no
      equivalent of `taskset`.
  refer sedentary.BenchmarkDevice
  entry .deploy   does nothing: the staged JAR is already local
  entry .invoke   runs the staged JAR with `java` on the local machine and returns its standard output
      Raises `Bench.Error` if the JVM cannot be started or exits with a nonzero status. See
      `BenchmarkDevice#invoke` for `heap`, `cpus` and `gc`.
  entry .undeploy   does nothing: the staged JAR is left where staging wrote it

entry .NetworkDevice   a benchmark device on a remote host, reached as a user over SSH
  detail
      `NetworkDevice(user, host)` copies each staged JAR to the user's home directory on
      `host` with `scp`, runs it there with `ssh` and `java`, and removes it afterwards. It
      relies on the local `ssh` and `scp` commands and the user's SSH configuration and keys:
      the connection must authenticate without a prompt.

      A remote Linux machine is the device to use for figures which must not be disturbed by
      the developer's own machine, and the only one on which a `cpus` limit pins the
      measurement JVM to cores, with `taskset`. Each operation pays an SSH handshake; a
      `session` pays it once:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val device = NetworkDevice(t"bench", host"bench.example.com")

      device.session: session ?=>
        given BenchmarkDevice = session
        Bench()
      ```

      The `NetworkDevice.sessional` given, in the companion, makes this possible, and needs a
      `Tactic[Bench.Error]` in scope for the opening of the connection.
  refer sedentary.NetworkDevice.Session
  refer sedentary.NetworkDeviceSessional
  refer sedentary.BenchmarkDevice
  entry .sessional   lets `device.session` open one multiplexed SSH connection to a `NetworkDevice`
      The `Sessional` instance for `NetworkDevice`, in implicit scope: a
      `NetworkDeviceSessional`, built with the `Tactic[Bench.Error]` in scope, which raises if
      the master connection cannot be opened.
  entry .Session   a benchmark device multiplexing every operation over one OpenSSH ControlMaster connection
    detail
        The device a `NetworkDevice`'s `session` lends to its block. It deploys, runs and
        removes JARs on the same host as its `NetworkDevice`, but through the control socket
        of a master connection opened once, so no operation pays an SSH handshake of its own.
        It is not constructed directly: the session opens the connection, lends the device,
        and closes the connection when the block ends, whether it returns or throws.
    entry #deploy   copies a staged JAR to the host over the shared connection
        Raises `Bench.Error` if the copy fails.
    entry #invoke   runs a staged JAR on the host over the shared connection, and returns its output
        As `NetworkDevice#invoke`, with no handshake of its own. Raises `Bench.Error`.
    entry #undeploy   removes a staged JAR from the host over the shared connection
        Raises `Bench.Error` if the removal fails.
  entry #user   the user name to log in to the host as
  entry #host   the remote host, as a `Hostname`
  entry #deploy   copies a staged JAR to the user's home directory on the host, with `scp`
      Raises `Bench.Error` if the copy fails.
  entry #invoke   runs a staged JAR on the host with `ssh` and `java`, and returns its output
      Raises `Bench.Error` if the connection fails or the JVM exits with a nonzero status. On
      the host, a `cpus` limit both sets the JVM's processor count and pins the process to
      that many cores with `taskset`, so the host must have it. See `BenchmarkDevice#invoke`.
  entry #undeploy   removes a staged JAR from the host, with `ssh`
      Raises `Bench.Error` if the removal fails.

entry .NetworkDeviceSessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens a scoped SSH session on a `NetworkDevice`
  detail
      A named instance class, not usually written by hand: `NetworkDevice.sessional` provides
      it, and `device.session` uses it. Its `#session` opens an OpenSSH ControlMaster
      connection to the host (authenticated once, with a control socket under `/tmp`), lends a
      `NetworkDevice.Session` to the block, and closes the master connection when the block
      ends, whether it returns or throws. It takes a `Tactic[Bench.Error]`, which is raised if
      the connection cannot be opened; a failure to close it is ignored.
  refer sedentary.NetworkDevice
  entry #Self   the target of a session: `NetworkDevice`
  entry #Result   what a session lends its block: a `NetworkDevice.Session`
  entry #session   opens a master connection, lends a `NetworkDevice.Session` to the block, and closes it
      Called through the `session` extension, `device.session: session ?=> …`, rather than
      directly. The block's result may leave the session; the session device itself should
      not, since its connection is closed when the block ends.

entry .OperationSize   the size of one benchmarked operation, which turns a time into a throughput rate
  detail
      A benchmark which processes data reports its rate — megabytes or characters per second
      — as well as its time per operation, when it is told how much each operation processes.
      The size is usually given as a quantity, which `.conversion` turns into an
      `OperationSize`:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      bench(m"gzip 4 MB")(target = 1*Second, operationSize = 4194304*Byte):
        '{ Compression.gzip(Corpus.large) }
      ```

      The size renders with a metric prefix (`4.2 MB`), and the rate likewise (`MB/s`). Per-cell
      sizes are given with `Bench.Plan#sized`, for a benchmark whose cells process different
      amounts of data, so that their rates are comparable where their times are not.
  refer sedentary.Bench.Plan
  refer probably.Benchmark
  entry .conversion   converts a `Quantity` into an `OperationSize`, for `Bench#apply`'s `operationSize`
      Any `Quantity` of a measure converts, with the `Decimalizer` in scope rendering both the
      size and, once the mean time is known, the rate, in the quantity's units per second. The
      given is `inline`, so the rendering is elaborated where the quantity is passed, with the
      caller's unit and prefix givens visible: a non-inline conversion would render `4194304*Byte`
      without its unit or prefix.
  entry #sizeText   the size of an operation, rendered for reading: `4.2 MB`
  entry #rateText   renders the rate, given the mean time of an operation in seconds: `120 MB/s`
  entry #value   the size as a number in its base unit (bytes or characters), for charting
